%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab: Elastic Collisions
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_13F91CCF] ## Part 1: Elastic Collision, Equal Mass
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_50C67419] ### Step 1. Conduct Experiment
%[text] ![Simulink Icon](text:image:4054) **Pro-tip.** Arrange your screen so you can view both dialogs at the same time.
%[text] 1. Open the Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackLab](<matlab:  airTrackLab>).
%[text] 2. Double-click the air track image to open the Multibody Explorer dialog. Configure the lab to use elastic collisions by pressing the button **1. Elastic**.
%[text] 3. Start the simulation.
%[text] 4. In the Simulink model dialog, give Cart 1 a push in $+x${"altText":"+x"} direction by pressing button **Pulse +x (0.1 sec)** in the Cart 1 Forces section.
%[text] 5. Stop the simulation once Cart 2 has passed through gate 2. \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select 1. Elastic to use elastic collisions.](text:image:5401)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select* ***1.*** ***Elastic*** *to use elastic collisions.*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_25CE59A2] ### Step 2: Record Measurements
%[text] 1. Record the amount of time cart 1 is detected by the sensor in gate 1.
%[text] 2. Record the amount of time cart 2 is detected by the sensor in gate 2.  \
%[text] **Note**: replace **NaN** with your result in each case.
% Record times for carts to pass through gates
t0_cart1_p1 = NaN;
t0_cart2_p1 = NaN;

% Record times for carts to pass through gates post-collision
tf_cart1_p1 = NaN;
tf_cart2_p1 = NaN;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04D211EF] ### Step 3: Calculate Velocities Before and After Collision
%[text] If you are not sure how to calculate the velocity from the stop clock times times, consult the [Pre-Lab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts/Prelab.m'))>) for an example. Recall that the cart is 0.15 m long.
% Calculate velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p1_cart1 = NaN
v0_p1_cart2 = NaN

% Calculate velocities after collision (in m/s)
vf_p1_cart1 = NaN
vf_p1_cart2 = NaN
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1B1F182A] ### Step 4: Analyze Momentum and Energy
%[text] Using the velocities calculated in step 3, calculate
%[text] 1. Total momentum before and after collision
%[text] 2. Percentage of momentum lost
%[text] 3. Total energy before and after collision
%[text] 4. Percentage of energy lost \
%[text] **Note**
%[text] - To compute the % loss use: $\\% \\text{loss} = \\frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \\times 100\\%${"altText":"% \text{loss} = \frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \times 100%"}
%[text] - Each cart has a mass of 180 g = 0.18 kg (+ any added mass).
%[text] - Give masses in terms of kilograms so that the momenta are given in kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m/s and energy is given in Joules (1 J = 1 kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m$^2${"altText":"^2"}/s$^2${"altText":"^2"}).
%[text] - If a quantity is conserved, it should be the same before and after the collision.
%[text] - These calculations help you see how close the result is to an ideal system. \
% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da1 = NaN
pf_da1 = NaN
% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da1 = NaN
ekf_da1 = NaN
% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da1 = NaN
% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da1 = NaN
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_84563180] ## Part 2: Elastic Collision, Unequal Masses
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_675520C2] ### Step 1. Conduct Experiment
%[text] 1. Add 200 g to cart 2. To do this, double-click on the air track model and set **Extra Mass, Cart 2** to **2 plates (200 g)**.
%[text] 2. Start the simulation.
%[text] 3. Give cart 1 a push in $+x${"altText":"+x"} direction by pressing button **Pulse +x (0.1 sec)** in the Cart 1 Forces section.
%[text] 4. Stop the simulation once Cart 1 has passed through gate 1 two times (at least 3 non-zero times should be shown on the top level of the model). \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F4DF744B] ### Step 2: Record Measurements
%[text] 1. Record the amount of time cart 1 is detected by the sensor in gate 1 (first pass).
%[text] 2. Record the amount of time cart 1 is detected by the sensor in gate 1 (second pass).
%[text] 3. Record the amount of time cart 2 is detected by the sensor in gate 2.  \
% Time required for cart 2 to pass through the gate as it approaches cart1:
t0_p2_cart1 = NaN;
tf_p2_cart1 = NaN;
tf_p2_cart2 = NaN;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0C1DC808] ### Step 3: Calculate Velocities Before and After Collision
%[text] If you are not sure how to calculate the velocity from the stop clock times times, consult the [Pre-Lab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Scripts/Prelab.m'))>) for an example. Recall that the cart is 0.15 m long.
% Velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p2_cart1 = NaN
v0_p2_cart2 = NaN
% Velocities after collision (in m/s)
vf_p2_cart1 = NaN
vf_p2_cart2 = NaN

%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6FB40353] ### Step 4: Analyze Momentum and Energy
%[text] Using the velocities calculated in step 3, calculate:
%[text] 1. Total momentum before and after collision
%[text] 2. Percentage of momentum lost
%[text] 3. Total energy before and after collision
%[text] 4. Percentage of energy lost \
%[text] **Note**
%[text] - These calculations help you see how close the result is to an ideal system.
%[text] - If a quantity is conserved, it should be the same before and after the collision.
%[text] - Each cart has a mass of 180 g = 0.18 kg (+ any added mass).
%[text] - Give masses in terms of kilograms so that the momenta are given in kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m/s and energy is given in Joules (1 J = 1 kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m$^2${"altText":"^2"}/s$^2${"altText":"^2"}).
%[text] - To compute the % loss use: $\\% \\text{loss} = \\frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \\times 100\\%${"altText":"% \text{loss} = \frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \times 100%"} \
% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da2 = NaN
pf_da2 = NaN
% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da2 = NaN
ekf_da2 = NaN
% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da2 = NaN
% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da2 = NaN
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%%
